  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 311 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe high continues to influence our weather through much of the \nremainder of the week as it drifts east into the Mid Atlantic \nRegion. Temperatures warm above normal Tuesday, and continue a slow \nwarming trend through the end of the week in persistent southerly \nflow on the western side of the ridge.  Global deterministic \nguidance continues to show a short wave trough moving off the \nRockies Wednesday and into the Midwest Wednesday night.  The GFS and \nECMWF are in better agreement this morning than in previous runs \nwith this wave and the attendant surface cold front Thursday.  The \nsurface ridge axis is forecast to extend across the deep south, \nblocking moisture return from The Gulf by late Wednesday into \nThursday.  Both models now show little if any precip reaching our \narea for lack of moisture return due to the blocking ridge as the \nfront moves into northern Missouri and central Illinois on Thursday \nbefore stalling.  The NBM has accordingly scaled back PoPs to less \nthan 20 percent across the area except for northeast Missouri. Fewer \nthan 20 percent of the LREF members are showing any precip for \nThursday and Friday with the front in the vicinity as well, which \nlends confidence to a dry forecast for that period.  The next \nupstream short wave moves across the Rockies into the Great Plains \nearly Saturday.  Models indicate this second wave will be stronger \nthan the first, resulting in a stronger low level cyclone and better \nmoisture return.  The ECMWF and GFS both show some modest low level \nmoisture convergence ahead of the low level wave across the Mid \nMississippi Valley, resulting in 1-2 tenths of an inch of QPF.  Both \nthe NBM and LREF also increase the probability for precip to 30-50 \npercent for much of the area, and up to 60 percent for parts of \nnortheast and central Missouri.",
